Yes, using three trees that descend from the same person—even if they don't match yours yet—is the Gold Standard for breaking through "brick walls." This is called Reverse Genealogy.
Since their trees don't match each other, they are likely stuck on different children of the same ancestor. Your goal is to find the convergent point.
The "Convergence" Matrix
Look at these specific spots on their trees to find where they eventually "meet."
| Where to Look | What it Tells You | Why it Matters |
|---|---|---|
| The "End of Line" | The furthest ancestor they found. | If all three stop at the same surname/county, that's your target. |
| Great-Grandparents | Their 2nd-3rd generation. | If you share 100cM+, your MRCA is likely their G-Grandparent. |
| Siblings of Ancestors | The "Aunts and Uncles." | One tree might list a sister who married into your known family. |
The "Triangulation" Workflow
If Tree A, Tree B, and Tree C don't match:
 * Ignore Surnames: Look for a shared location and year. (e.g., All three have ancestors in St. Paul, MN in 1890).
 * Build "Floating Trees": Start a new, private tree. Put the "End of Line" person from all three matches into it.
 * Research Upward: Use census records to see if those three "End of Line" people lived near each other or had parents with the same name.
Python Tree Convergence Tool
This script helps you identify the "Target Era" where these three trees should theoretically meet based on their highest cM.
def find_convergence_target(cm_list, avg_birth_year=1960):
    # Use the highest cM to find the closest possible connection
    top_cm = max(cm_list)
    
    if top_cm > 400:
        gen_back = 3 # Great-Grandparents
    elif 100 < top_cm <= 400:
        gen_back = 4 # 2x Great-Grandparents
    else:
        gen_back = 5 # 3x Great-Grandparents
        
    target_year = avg_birth_year - (gen_back * 30)
    
    return f"""
    --- TARGET DATA ---
    Highest Cluster Match: {top_cm} cM
    Estimated Generations to MRCA: {gen_back}
    Focus Search Year: Around {target_year}
    Action: Look for a couple in this era that links all 3 trees.
    """

# Example: Your 3 matches are 250cM, 150cM, and 90cM
print(find_convergence_target([250, 150, 90]))

Where to look if they don't match each other:
 * The Maiden Names: Often, one tree tracks the father’s line and the other tracks the mother’s. Look for the wives in their trees.
 * The Census: Look at the 1880 or 1900 Federal Census for the "End of Line" ancestors. Are they living in the same ward or township?
